        {
          "say": "Move the answer descriptions to different positions and keep their semantic identities attached. A robust decision should follow the description supported by the evidence. In a complete causal model, changing serialized order can change hidden vectors too. Test the full request and align outputs by option identity.",
          "start": 0.4,
          "end": 19.264,
          "duration": 18.864,
          "until": 20.064
        },
        {
          "say": "Drop C. Keep both remaining scores fixed. B's share grows from about twenty-four and a half percent to almost twenty-seven percent. Look at the two fractions. B has the same weight on top. Removing C took one unit out of the total on the bottom.",
          "start": 20.064,
          "end": 37.239000000000004,
          "duration": 17.175,
          "until": 38.039
        },
        {
          "say": "Ask two questions about the same log. Which cause should we investigate? Did the tests start? Kev reads the log once. Each question starts in its own row from that cached state. The recurrent layers carry information forward, so an attention mask alone would let one question affect the other. Add an Unknown choice to the second row. Check that the first row's scores still match its original scores within numerical tolerance.",
          "start": 38.039,
          "end": 65.429,
          "duration": 27.39,
          "until": 66.429
        }
